A||||6||JohnF||Sunroof leaking?||||12||
Z||000000||JohnF||10-20-2001||01:30 PM||||For general info - <P>I rebuilt my removeable roof this a.m.  ALL new hardware and gaskets (fore & aft/top & bottom).  A leaking roof on the driver's side and at the rear hinge now NO LONGER leaks.  <P>So, contrary to popular opinion, a Fiero roof a not supposed to leak - they CAN be fixed!||||reg||12
Z||000001||Jncomutt||10-20-2001||08:03 PM||||How much did it end up costin ya to do everything???||||reg||1
Z||000002||JohnF||10-20-2001||08:59 PM||||How about $15.00 and 15 minutes!!  That was 2 lower gaskets, 4 seals, 4 top screws. 4 bottom screws, 2 "hinges".  <P>Gathering the miscellaneous parts from 3 different dealerships and one internet address was the challenge.<P>Unbelievably cheap.  Now, I can replace the headliner that is water "challenged"! And, I can install my sunshade without worry!||||reg||1
Z||000003||beer_stud_21||10-20-2001||09:59 PM||||Wow, that is cheap!  Could you list the part numbers for those things so we would have an easier time finding them?  My sunroof "hinges" (the tabs at the front) are really rusty. One has completely rusted away to nothing.  If I could fix all this for $15.00 that would be awsome!<P>Thanks,<BR>Marlin||||reg||1
Z||000004||JohnF||10-21-2001||07:46 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by beer_stud_21:<BR><B>Could you list the part numbers...</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Better yet, go to any GM part counter and tell them you want the parts.  They will look up the exploded view on their computer and list the available parts (the 2 that go on the car roof itself aren't!).  They will provide you with a print out with the P/N if, you want.  They will also supply you with the parts or tell you what dealership has them in your area.  <P>I would up going to 3 different dealers and ordering one part from the warehouse to get them all together - still no big expense!<P>If you still want p/n of all, send me pvt e-mail.<P>||||reg||7
Z||000005||FieroSoul||10-21-2001||08:42 AM||||  It could be easier than that, depends on what parts are bad.  I replaced my gasket($20) then made my own seals out of an old bicycle inner tube for the screws, added a little RTV in the holes to be sure and viola. No leak.  Because of the thickness of the inner tube seal under the square latch part it is now a very tight seal (added benny).  Cost was about the same with similar results, just not a lot of condescending dealer parts aggravation. ||||reg||6
Z||000006||ur2biteme||10-22-2001||12:13 AM||||could be even cheaper than that.  i saw once in a junkyard where the previous owner caulked the damn thing shut!  no respect &lt;shakes head&gt;||||reg||1
